#!/usr/bin/env -S mos build --open

#set document(
  title: "Hello, Mosaic",
  author: "Mosaic contributors",
  language: "en",
)

#set page(
  paper: "A4",
  margin: 24mm,
  numbering: "bottom-center",
)

#set text(
  font: "Noto Sans",
  size: 11pt,
  leading: 1.35,
)

= Dear LaTeX,

It has been ***eleven years***. Eleven years of `\begin{document}`,
of mysterious vertical-mode errors, of citations rendered as
`[?]` because BibTeX had Opinions about the .bib file I had not
yet finished saving. Eleven years of you greeting every PDF I
make with the same Computer Modern serif that everyone,
everywhere, immediately recognises as *a sad student wrote
this*.

I have moved on. The new arrangement embeds **real** TrueType
outlines, Noto Sans in this case, through a Type 0 CID font
with a /ToUnicode CMap, which is a sentence I should not have to
know, but here we are. Copy-paste round-trips. Search works. The
Polish co-author gets **Łódź** instead of *L*, a space, and an
apostrophe. The Czech reviewer gets **Příliš žluťoučký kůň**
without three preamble lines of `\usepackage{}` ritual. The
Russian correspondents get **Привет, Мозаика**. The Greek
desk gets **Καλημέρα, κόσμε**. None of them get an email from
me asking which encoding their text editor was using when they
sent the draft.

The math, fine, you were good at math. I will give you that. But
I am also done feeling like a fraud for pasting a single ≤ into
a paragraph and being forced to load amsmath, amssymb, and a
third package whose name I have forgotten because the universe
took it from me. Mosaic shapes math operators (≤ ≥ ≠ √ ∂ ∑)
through automatic per-glyph fallback to the bundled Noto Sans
Math cut, mid-word, mid-sentence, with no `#set text(font: ...)`,
no `\usepackage{}`, no `$ … $`. The body face does not contain
them; the fallback face does; the layout engine notices on its
own and routes the cluster. The result reads like prose because
it *is* prose; ∂f/∂x ≠ 0 sits in this line the same way "the"
does.

My grievances, in no particular order except the one I wrote
them in:

5. The error messages. **Especially** the one about `\par`.
9. The figure handling. One `#figure(image: "...", caption: "...")` block here, and forty minutes of `\begin{figure}[!htbp]` float-placement bargaining there. I have aged out of caring which letters do which.
27. The fi/fl ligatures, which you handled but never let me actually *see* without diagnostic incantations. Mine ship as one glyph in the PDF the way the typographer intended. Copy "figment" or "flicker" out of this document and paste it somewhere; the ligature collapses back into the source codepoints on the way out. It is shaping, not a font hack.

Sub-grievances, indented because that is also a thing now:

- The way you treated quotation marks. We have em dashes — like
  that — without three hyphens, and no, I will not be installing
  a package for it.
- The way `verbatim` and `code` could never quite agree about
  whitespace.
  - Yes, I know it was the package's fault.
  - I do not care whose fault it was.
- The bibliography. I am still angry about the bibliography.

== Some good news

*Italic* still means italic. **Bold** still means bold. They
route through the real designed cuts of Noto Sans, not a faux-
slant transformation, and the same is true in Cyrillic
(*курсив*, **жирный**) and Greek (*πλάγια*, **έντονα**). If
anyone needs the Adobe Core 14 fonts (you, mostly, when you are
nostalgic), `#set text(font: "Helvetica")` is still there. It
will look exactly like the 1985 you remember.

I am attaching a screenshot of what the README promised, so we
can compare it later with what shipped. You will notice it does
not say `\documentclass{article}` anywhere.

#figure(
  image: "demo.png",
  caption: "Exhibit A. A PNG, appearing here, below the sentence that references it, as God intended and LaTeX refused.",
)

Yours, with twenty-three open browser tabs of Stack Exchange
threads from 2007,

Mosaic
